WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.970 --> 00:00:02.240
Since the dawn of man,

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.032
meat has been on the menu.

00:00:04.032 --> 00:00:06.128
A pure beef hamburger.

00:00:06.128 --> 00:00:06.961
♪ Chick-Fil-A ♪

00:00:06.961 --> 00:00:08.030
Over a pound of meat.

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:09.890
We have the meat.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:11.600
And even with growing trends

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.460
of vegetarian diets and
plant-based substitutes,

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:17.310
around 89% of the world population

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.290
consider themselves omnivores.

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:20.990
We have come a long way

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:23.470
from our hunter and gatherer roots.

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:26.860
We have managed to
establish a whole industry

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:30.570
designed to feed almost
7.7 billion people.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.010
We've maximized production

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:34.130
and we've managed to reduce hunger

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:36.220
over the past couple of decades.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.760
And although we've become
exceedingly efficient

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.160
at putting meat on the table,

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.570
raising livestock animals by the billions

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.050
comes with its own set of consequences.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:47.150
If animal agriculture

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:49.270
continues to expand the way it has

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:53.610
there's a risk that we won't
be able to stop climate change.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:55.710
But with rapid advances in technology,

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:57.720
we might soon see a food industry

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.853
that leaves all those problems behind.

00:01:11.131 --> 00:01:12.131
I'm about to be

00:01:12.131 --> 00:01:15.110
one of the world's first
commercial customers

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:17.490
for lab grown meat, cultured meat.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:20.643
To Eat Just is the company
and I'm very, very excited.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:24.423
Here is the chicken.

00:01:25.349 --> 00:01:26.763
Looks pretty crispy outside.

00:01:31.387 --> 00:01:32.290
It's really good.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:34.800
I mean look it's really tasty.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:36.910
Here at 1880 in Singapore,

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:38.610
cultured chicken is being served

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.310
under a regulatory approval

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:42.880
granted by the Singaporean government.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.720
It's the very first time
a cultured meat product,

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.010
meat made from animal cells grown in a lab

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:50.163
has been approved for commercial sale.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:53.010
Eat Just, the company behind

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:54.610
these chicken nugget style dishes

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:56.480
is based out of San Francisco

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.200
and got their start in 2011

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.460
developing plant-based egg substitutes,

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.270
like mung bean scrambled
eggs and mayonnaise.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.410
But it wasn't until 2017

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:07.230
that they announced
they were shifting focus

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:08.803
to a cultured meat product.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:13.010
In order to feed the world that we live in

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:14.630
a lot animal protein,

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:17.890
we need to take a third today
and it'll be more tomorrow

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:18.820
of our world

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:22.160
and make it dedicated to
feeding chickens and cows.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:25.167
We go to areas that are filled
with biodiverse rainforests,

00:02:25.167 --> 00:02:28.400
taking a bulldozer to them
and planting soy and corn

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.500
to feed the chickens that we eat.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.210
Climate change is accelerated

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:35.000
by the kind of animal
production that we engage in.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.470
When we bring animals
in tiny little spaces,

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.920
it accelerates the probability
that more zoonotic diseases

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.070
are gonna impact our families.

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:45.050
And from a morality perspective,

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:48.640
why do we need to cause any
harm to any other living thing

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.610
to have dinner that tastes good?

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:53.110
It does not have to be that way.

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:56.810
And those things underlie
the force of this idea.

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:57.990
The meat alternatives

00:02:57.990 --> 00:02:59.710
that have made it to the market thus far

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:01.110
have been plant-based,

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:02.700
imitation beef and pork

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.710
made from ingredients like
pea protein and coconut oil

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:08.553
by companies like Beyond
Meat and Impossible Foods.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.190
That's a very different
proposition from cultured meat,

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:14.930
which is made from actual animal cells.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:18.240
I'm a huge fan of any
company, of any person,

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.230
trying to make a product that's
improving the food system,

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:23.400
including all the plant-based proteins.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:24.620
Maybe in some future menu,

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:27.020
you'll have this kind
of cultivated chicken

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:28.910
and you'll also have
a plant-based version.

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:29.910
That's fine.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:32.330
But I guess you have to ask
yourself in our lifetime

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:36.720
is that diner likely to also
remove the conventional option

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:38.130
on the menu too?

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:40.090
We do think we need a
more permanent solution.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:42.280
We do think we sort of need, if you will,

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:44.060
an end game to this problem.

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:45.620
And we just think this is more likely

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:47.293
to be the path to achieve that.

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:52.317
So animal cell technology

00:03:52.317 --> 00:03:55.100
has been around for several decades

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:58.700
for applications such as
development of vaccines

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.829
and other biotherapeutics.

00:04:00.829 --> 00:04:04.410
The first known prototype
of cultured meats

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:08.100
was done by Mark Post earlier in 2011.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:11.460
And essentially he was
developing a beef patty.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.450
It was a very expensive prototype.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:17.650
If I'm not mistaken above
250,000 Euros at the time

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:19.570
to produce just one beef patty.

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:21.470
It was more of a proof of concept

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:24.740
to really demonstrate that
this technology is possible

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:26.583
and that it can be done.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:31.410
The way that we grow and
produce cultured chicken

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:34.170
is essentially we isolate chicken cells

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:36.590
from the chickens without
having to kill them,

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:39.043
so from a small biopsy, for instance.

00:04:40.008 --> 00:04:42.720
We immerse these cells in a liquid broth

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:46.230
that contains the nutrients
that the cells need to survive

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:48.430
and be healthy and to grow.

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:50.510
We really need these cells to multiply,

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:52.710
to reach the numbers that are sufficient

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:54.373
to make a meat product.

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:57.770
We're looking into the cells

00:04:57.770 --> 00:04:59.720
that are really important for meat.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.850
So we think about muscle cells, fat cells.

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:04.050
By that isolation,

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:08.343
we are able to form those
muscle tissues in culture.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:13.870
The culture happens inside
of a fermentation tank,

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:15.620
what we call the bioreactor,

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:17.660
which is essentially providing

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:20.140
the same conditions as the animal body,

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:24.310
warm temperatures, some sort
of mechanical stimulation,

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:28.040
so that the cells are exposed
to very similar conditions

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:30.900
that they have inside of the animal.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:34.670
After the cells proliferate
and reach a certain density,

00:05:34.670 --> 00:05:36.690
the final step is essentially

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:38.720
to separate those cells

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:41.120
from the liquid broth that they are in.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.160
We just make sure that the product

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:46.490
meets our quality and safety standards,

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:49.253
and then those cells are
ready for consumption.

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:54.470
Milk, cheese, yogurt,

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:57.000
a lot of those derived from fermentations.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.750
The only difference is
that they are starting

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:00.580
from a different cell type.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:02.820
In this case, we're just
using the animal cells

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:05.933
as the initial and
beginning of the product.

00:06:10.117 --> 00:06:12.620
Eat Just has developed
chicken nuggets so far

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:14.390
with far more textured chicken breasts

00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:16.310
planned for the future.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:19.060
But another cell-based
meat startup Memphis Meats,

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:20.530
has already started working

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:22.980
on culturing other animal cells as well.

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:23.900
We've done beef,

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:24.733
we've done duck,

00:06:24.733 --> 00:06:25.970
we've done chicken.

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:27.630
And these are things that are favorites

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:28.780
in various parts of the world.

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:31.080
For instance, people in America

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:32.630
love beef and chicken.

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:34.210
People in China love duck.

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:36.900
So we wanted to show
that we at Memphis Meats

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:39.870
can produce meats that
we as a global population

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:41.280
love to eat.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:43.210
There's a lot of similarity
in how we grow the meats

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:45.150
from various animals or fish,

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:46.240
but they're also different

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:47.730
because of nutrients they need

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:49.830
and how they actually come together

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:51.530
to form the final product, the meat.

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:52.600
And I think it's very similar

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:54.550
to how we also grow various animals.

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:56.300
There's similarities in the feed

00:06:56.300 --> 00:06:58.740
that we give cows and pigs and chickens,

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:00.520
but there's also
differences how we cultivate

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:02.480
and it's very similar for us as well.

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:04.950
So we've been working
with the FDA and the USDA

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:06.200
for the last three years

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:07.840
at really developing the guidelines

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:09.500
on how this food should be regulated

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:10.670
when we come to the market.

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:13.770
As soon as we get regulatory
approval in the United States

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:15.510
people that will be able
to go to their supermarket

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:17.100
and buy it and enjoy it,

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:18.740
they'll be able to go to
a restaurant near them

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:20.986
and order meat directly from animal cells.

00:07:20.986 --> 00:07:23.600
But it's not just getting the meat right.

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:25.220
Once approved, the gates open

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:27.400
to a very high potential demand

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:30.120
and scaling becomes the next big hurdle.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.360
Before we could only make
chicken for myself, my friends,

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:35.260
my girlfriend, my family, my colleagues.

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:36.580
Now we actually have the ability

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:38.390
to make chicken for millions of people.

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:39.860
So that allows us to invest

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:43.340
and to allow the economies
of scale to drop the price.

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:45.340
The second is we need to reduce the cost

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:47.970
of the nutrients that
these cells are consuming.

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:49.200
So I think chicken feed

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:51.324
raises or drops the price of chicken.

00:07:51.324 --> 00:07:53.950
The nutrients we feed
ourselves does the same thing.

00:07:53.950 --> 00:07:55.290
So we need to drive that down.

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:57.640
The cell density is
another technical element.

00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:00.010
We need to continue to increase that.

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:01.380
And hammering all those things

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:03.920
over the course of the
next five, 10, 15 years

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:04.990
will put us in a place

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:07.400
where we're significantly below the cost

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:08.830
of this animal protein

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:11.070
and that will be one of
the main inflection points

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:13.220
that will lead to a
new way of eating meat.

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:19.920
So here is a closeup of a
half-eaten crispy chicken bite.

00:08:21.565 --> 00:08:22.767
You know what?

00:08:22.767 --> 00:08:26.600
What I'm really thinking
is just I'm eating chicken.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:28.065
I like chicken.

00:08:28.065 --> 00:08:30.650
You know, the normal chickens eat,

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:32.260
I don't know where they came from.

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:33.810
And so I don't know why
I should be very stressed

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:35.210
where this came from either.

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:40.390
The meat market is worth

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:42.830
more than one trillion dollars annually.

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:46.340
And there are over 60 startups
in the cultured meat field.

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:49.910
Cultured meat has attracted
several prominent investors,

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:54.540
including Sergey Brin, Richard
Branson, and Bill Gates.

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:57.320
What is also interesting
is that even meat giants,

00:08:57.320 --> 00:09:02.300
such as Cargill and Tyson
have invested in the space.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:07.300
It's this disruptive
potential of the market

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:11.060
as well as the promise to greatly

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:13.930
reduce the climate
impact of meat production

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:17.350
that has attracted investors.

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:18.650
It remains to be seen

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:21.730
who will get regulatory
approval in which countries

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:23.900
and whether consumers
will like lab grown meat

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:25.570
enough to make the switch.

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:26.940
And while there's no evidence

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:27.773
that lab grown meat

00:09:27.773 --> 00:09:29.970
is any less healthy than traditional meat,

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:31.240
there's an ongoing debate

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:34.150
over how healthy eating
meat is in general.

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:36.170
But if these products are a success

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:38.670
they could be the
beginning of a more humane,

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:41.150
less destructive meat industry.

00:09:41.150 --> 00:09:42.770
The food and ag industry

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:44.840
is the most innovative
industry in the world.

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:47.560
It's always figured out how
to feed a growing population

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:49.290
and we've never failed doing so.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:51.810
And it's been very clear to
us that we are part of that

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:52.643
and we're gonna figure out

00:09:52.643 --> 00:09:55.230
how to feed the next three
billion people joining us

00:09:55.230 --> 00:09:57.360
and I just see an evolution.

00:09:57.360 --> 00:09:59.340
We can do this a bit differently.

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:02.350
We can do this in a way that is consistent

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:04.612
with the kind of people
that we want to be,

00:10:04.612 --> 00:10:06.370
the kind of planet that
we all want to live in.

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:08.020
We can build a food system

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:11.760
that reflects really the best
characteristics of who we are

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:13.423
and that makes me really hopeful.

